la_bench: does a change make libarchive faster or slower?

libarchive has tests for what it does, but nothing that measures how fast it
does it.  These tools compare two builds, format by format, and say whether a
difference is real.  They use only libarchive's public API, so they can compare
any two versions, and they stay outside both trees being compared.

	la_bench.c	one operation, once, in its own process
	build.sh	builds la_bench against a libarchive source tree
	compare.sh	runs two builds in turn
	compare.awk	reports the differences from compare.sh's results
	test.sh		tests the above

Usage:

	./build.sh ~/libarchive-a /tmp/bench-a
	./build.sh ~/libarchive-b /tmp/bench-b
	./compare.sh -o /tmp/results /tmp/bench-a /tmp/bench-b
	./test.sh /tmp/bench-a

Before trusting a comparison, compare two separate builds of the same source.
That shows how much the machine moves on its own: a difference no bigger than
that one isn't a difference.  Run them on an idle machine, plugged in; a machine
doing other work mostly shows up as "unclear" results.

Operations:

	list	reads every header and skips the data
	read	reads every header and all the data
	write	creates the archive, sending the output nowhere

Reads run from memory and writes go nowhere, so disks don't enter into it.  The
clock covers everything from creating the archive object to freeing it.

Reading the report:

	worse	B is past the threshold (default 1%), with confidence
	better	the same, in the other direction
	same	B is within the threshold, with confidence
	unclear	it could be either: more rounds might settle it

"wall" and "cpu" are time, "rss" is the process's peak memory, and "size" is the
archive written.  Peak memory includes the input held in memory and, for write,
the data to be written, so compare the change between builds, not the amount.

compare.awk exits 2 if the builds read different numbers of entries or bytes,
because their timings aren't then measuring the same work.

To add a format, add a row to formats[] in la_bench.c, and enable any library it
needs in build.sh.

Limits:

 * POSIX only: Windows would need other clocks and another way to get peak
   memory.
 * Results compare two builds on one machine at one time.  They don't carry over
   to another machine or compare formats with each other.
 * A compiled change can shift code alignment and move timings a little without
   the code being any better or worse.  Very small differences can be that, which
   is why the threshold exists.
